<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Helvetic Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1798 CE – 1803 CE · capital Aarau; later Lucerne, Bern</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's centralised sister republic in Switzerland, which abolished the old cantons and subject lands and introduced equal citizenship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French troops invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy</a> in early 1798, and Bern fell in March. On 12 April 1798 a constitution drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ochs">Peter Ochs</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> proclaimed the Helvetic Republic, one and indivisible, with its first capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aarau">Aarau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic abolished the subject territories and created equal citizenship, freedom of religion and the press, and a single currency, the franc. Its directory and legislature replaced the old cantons with administrative units. Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-albert-stapfer">Philipp Albert Stapfer</a> planned public schooling. But French requisitions, conscription and the suppression of resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> made it unpopular, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Lucerne</a> and then Bern.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Repeated coups and a federalist uprising in 1802 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> to impose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> in 1803, restoring cantons in a new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation</a>. Its legacy includes the equality of cantons and the abolition of subject lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/helvetic-republic.jpg" alt="Helvetic Republic" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Omarcoz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Helvetic_Republic_(German).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1798 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aarau; later Lucerne, Bern</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Ochs, Frédéric-César de La Harpe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, French, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>République helvétique, Helvetische Republik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=helvetic-republic" aria-label="Helvetic Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 516 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Helvetic Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5188 772L5186 782M5188 772L5203 787M5188 772L5203 783M5188 772L5195 787M5188 772L5195 782M5188 772L5200 772M5188 772L5213 782M5188 772L5200 778M5188 772L5175 785M5188 772L5168 789M5188 772L5177 777M5188 772L5177 767M5188 772L5201 762M5188 772L5221 771M5188 772L5220 773M5188 772L5219 771M5188 772L5219 771M5188 772L5224 788M5188 772L5174 807M5188 772L5174 807M5188 772L5198 789M5188 772L5054 728M5188 772L5175 785"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5203 787h0M5203 783h0M5195 787h0M5195 782h0M5200 772h0M5213 782h0M5200 778h0M5175 785h0M5168 789h0M5177 777h0M5177 767h0M5201 762h0M5221 771h0M5220 773h0M5219 771h0M5219 771h0M5224 788h0M5174 807h0M5174 807h0M5198 789h0M5054 728h0M5175 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5188 772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aarau. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=helvetic-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
